WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.780
To get started, I just wanted to
ask you both to introduce yourself.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.350
We are really fortunate today to have
two second year students with us,

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.470
Petra Weiss and Mike Ding.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.100
Thank you again for joining us.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.940
Could both of you talk a little
bit about your backgrounds?

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.580
How did you come to the GSB?

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.940
Why did decide to pursue an MBA and
what were you doing before you came here?

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.070
Sure, yeah, so I'll, should I start?

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:25.410
&gt;&gt; Yeah, please.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.470
&gt;&gt; Great.
So I am Petra, for

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:30.050
those of you who didn't know, I'm Petra.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:34.420
And I worked at Google
before business school.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.650
And while I was there, I realized that I
had this gap in people management skills.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:43.110
And that was my reason for
wanting to go back to business school.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.770
And so as I was looking at schools I

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:49.430
was trying to find a school
that aligned with that goal.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.310
So one that focused on coaching and
interpersonal dynamics and

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.620
really building out those
people management skills.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.070
And as I learned more and

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.450
more about the GSB I saw that
that was almost a perfect match.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:06.220
And so I decided to go to business school
after about three years in the working

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:08.460
world to build out those skills.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.100
And to go back into a management role,
post business school.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.170
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.370
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; Mike, how about you?

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:18.430
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone, my name is Mike Ding,
I'm a second year MBA.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.590
Before Stanford,
I worked in private equity and

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:25.630
venture capital investing in
technology companies in San Francisco.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:32.410
So in terms of coming to business school,
I think one reason why I decided to come.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:39.070
Is my first job out of college, I actually
had a manager who was a Stanford MBA grad.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:46.325
The place where I worked was not
the most touchy feely sort of place.

00:01:46.325 --> 00:01:48.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; People tended to be very aggressive,

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.850
there wasn't a lot of mentorship.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:55.065
The type of leadership was very different
from what we're taught at the GSB.

00:01:55.065 --> 00:01:59.806
One thing that really inspired me,
in my first employer,

00:01:59.806 --> 00:02:04.955
was how this guy took a very
active interest in my development.

00:02:04.955 --> 00:02:10.454
And he led in a way that was very
motivational, inspiring, very empathetic.

00:02:10.454 --> 00:02:14.970
And I just really respected him,
I admired the way that he managed.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:19.780
And I wanted to learn how to do that
as well, and he actually encouraged me

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.530
to apply to the GSB was actually
one of my recommenders as well.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:27.570
&gt;&gt; Awesome, that's great,
that actually brings up a question

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.490
one of you is asking online
about the Alumni Network.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:35.660
So you worked with someone in
the GSB community pre MBA.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:39.260
How have you leveraged that network
currently, and that goes for both of you,

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.020
whether it's through internships or
general mentorship?

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:44.650
&gt;&gt; Do you want to take it?

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.023
&gt;&gt; Sure,
I can talk a little bit about that.

00:02:46.023 --> 00:02:51.040
So I have worked at a number of firms

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.372
that have been led by GSBers.

00:02:55.372 --> 00:02:59.760
The private equity firmed that I worked
at before business school was founded by

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:00.291
a GSBer.

00:03:00.291 --> 00:03:04.763
I worked at an investment firm in New York
over the summer that was also founded by

00:03:04.763 --> 00:03:05.311
a GSBer.

00:03:05.311 --> 00:03:09.952
And in each case,
when I receive an offer from these places,

00:03:09.952 --> 00:03:16.065
throughout the process I'd actually
been able to contact these individuals.

00:03:16.065 --> 00:03:18.498
Because they were affiliated with the GSB,

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:21.335
because they took an active
interest in students.

00:03:21.335 --> 00:03:25.190
Who were interested in the GSB, or
were currently attending the GSB.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:30.938
And in each case, I think the application
process itself was already

00:03:30.938 --> 00:03:36.205
very much helped by the fact that
I was a part of the GSB network.

00:03:36.205 --> 00:03:40.178
But even while I was there,
I think because I came from Stanford,

00:03:40.178 --> 00:03:43.734
because the founders of these
firms were Stanford grads.

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:48.086
It enabled me to connect with them much
more easily than someone who may not

00:03:48.086 --> 00:03:50.130
have had that shared background.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.854
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and building on that,
I think being at the GSB,

00:03:54.854 --> 00:03:58.839
I've used the Alumni Network
a huge amount.

00:03:58.839 --> 00:04:00.550
So for those you who don't know,

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:04.460
we have a joint degree program
in education and business.

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:08.460
So while I knew I wanted to go
back into people management after.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:12.150
I wasn't sure exactly whether that
would fall into the education space or

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.540
the tech space.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:17.956
And so I have done probably
about a hundred informational

00:04:17.956 --> 00:04:22.096
interviews with folks,
both at the GSB on the staff,

00:04:22.096 --> 00:04:26.972
and also alumni who have gone
into all sectors of education, so

00:04:26.972 --> 00:04:30.385
edtech, higher ed, and charter schools.

00:04:30.385 --> 00:04:35.288
And basically everything, and have used
those informationals to really help

00:04:35.288 --> 00:04:38.490
me figure out where I
belonged in those two fields.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:43.110
And so the alumni have been hugely
helpful in getting me to where I am,

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.980
and helping me figure out what's next for
me.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:47.310
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that is huge, wow.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:53.510
So you talked a little bit about
doing exploration in the MBA program,

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.310
pivoting a little bit from
where you were before.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.520
What was the cost benefit that you
thought about coming into an MBA program?

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.380
Was it hard to step out of the workplace?

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:06.760
What were the opportunity
costs that you thought about,

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:11.139
and especially when it came to
maybe family or significant others?

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.900
Sure, yeah, I think there were a couple
of big opportunity costs for me.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:20.090
The first, I would say,
is that my trajectory at Google,

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:24.050
where I was before school,
was looking positive.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:28.300
So it was really hard for
me to say if I do want to come back and

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:33.950
be a manager in tech,
why am I really taking this time out now?

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:38.975
When it looks like I could get to where
I want to get just by staying at Google.

00:05:38.975 --> 00:05:40.690
But a lot of my mentors, and

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:45.613
a lot of actually folks at Google that I
worked with had taken those two years and

00:05:45.613 --> 00:05:51.062
sort of reinforced the interpersonal and
coaching skills that you simply can't get,

00:05:51.062 --> 00:05:54.140
or invest in the same way
while you're working.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:56.250
And so, I figured for me,

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.550
it was really important to go
back early after three years.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:05.750
Before that opportunity cost got too high
for me, to restart my career a little bit.

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:09.420
And then again also I think because I
wanted to explore education as well.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.960
It seemed like a good
time to really decide,

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.270
do I continue to invest
in the tech space or not?

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:19.083
So it seemed like an opportunity cost but
when I stepped back, didn't feel as much

00:06:19.083 --> 00:06:22.805
one where it seemed like going
early would sort of reduce that.

00:06:22.805 --> 00:06:27.465
And then I would say the second big thing
is my fiance lives in New York City.

00:06:27.465 --> 00:06:29.600
And so that was a big decision,

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.365
because I had the opportunity to move with
Google to New York City, and be with him.

00:06:35.365 --> 00:06:38.364
Or obviously come to Stanford and
be apart.

00:06:38.364 --> 00:06:43.749
And so I think that was
definitely a huge consideration.

00:06:43.749 --> 00:06:47.737
But the GSP has a fantastic,
significant other network, and

00:06:47.737 --> 00:06:49.960
has really brought him in.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:52.940
He's actually really good friends
with Mike now through the GSB, so

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:56.510
he's been extremely welcomed
into this community.

00:06:56.510 --> 00:07:01.620
And he actually has had lots of interviews
with GSB alums that I've set up for

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.260
him through the Alumni Network as well.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:07.750
I actually, quick story,
emailed about ten people

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:13.200
in real estate in New York City on
his behalf, and all ten replied.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:13.730
&gt;&gt; Wow.
&gt;&gt; So

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:17.340
just another story about
the power of the Alumni Network.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:21.571
And yes, it's an opportunity cost,
but I think we'll be stronger for

00:07:21.571 --> 00:07:24.404
having had the exposure
we've had while here.

00:07:24.404 --> 00:07:28.495
&gt;&gt; That's great, speaking of the exposure
you've had while you're here.

00:07:28.495 --> 00:07:31.647
I was wondering if each of you could
talk a little bit about the academic

00:07:31.647 --> 00:07:32.294
experience.

00:07:32.294 --> 00:07:35.441
Mike, if you want to start with
maybe a favorite class, or

00:07:35.441 --> 00:07:39.519
an experience that stands out to you
specifically around the academics?

00:07:39.519 --> 00:07:44.032
&gt;&gt; Sure, well as I mentioned before,
one of the reasons why I came to

00:07:44.032 --> 00:07:49.121
business school was to really learn
how to be this type of inspirational,

00:07:49.121 --> 00:07:53.984
motivational leader that my first
manager was coming out of college.

00:07:53.984 --> 00:08:01.576
And I think the GSP does an incredible
job of giving you real life experience.

00:08:01.576 --> 00:08:07.892
Quote unquote, real life, but experiential
learning opportunities to do that.

00:08:07.892 --> 00:08:13.076
One class that I will remember
as the most impactful class that

00:08:13.076 --> 00:08:18.910
I had was interpersonal dynamics,
also known as touchy feely.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:26.550
Which is a class where you really explore
kind of how you react to other people.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:29.780
But also how you come off to other people.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:35.050
And I would say,
before the GSB, I was always

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:40.810
very afraid to truly open up
to people about how I felt,

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:45.430
how they made me feel, what my impact was
on them, what their impact was on me.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:50.050
But this is a sort of class
that creates a safe space for

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:54.510
people to really ask those
tough questions of each other.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:59.250
And, give each other honest
feedback about those impacts.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:01.730
And so, I would say that
the experiential learning,

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:05.040
especially through classes
like Touchy Feely,

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.510
has been the just most amazing part
of the academic curriculum here.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:10.310
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:12.220
Patrick, could you expand
a little bit on that?

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:15.540
What proportion would you say of
classes kind of break down different

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.080
teaching styles?

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:19.660
How much time would you spend
on cases versus lectures,

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:21.250
versus experiential learning?

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:22.810
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:26.050
&gt;&gt; And a little bit more on the tactical
side, is it hard to get electives, or

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.250
do you get to take
the classes that you want?

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:29.430
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:36.090
Yeah, so I would say first year was
more lecture and case based for me.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:37.860
And feel free to jump in if you disagree.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.850
But first year it was
probably about half and half.

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:46.920
So for some of my classes like Strategy,
we were in a case based format.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:50.780
For others, like Accounting,
it was a little bit more lecture based,

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:53.230
although there were some
cases that were done.

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:57.220
And so the professors basically
decided what would be the best way for

00:09:57.220 --> 00:10:01.130
folks to learn the material that we're
teaching and the most effective way.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:02.900
So that was mostly about half and half.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:06.680
For the elective second year, a lot
more of them have been experiential for

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:10.470
me because again,
I have been focusing on coaching, and

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:15.620
interpersonal dynamics, and
part of a program called Aruckle Fellows.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:20.960
And so that shift second year has
been mostly to experiential and

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:26.020
then still some lecture and
case base mixed in, but probably about 60%

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:31.000
experiential and
the rest case and lecture based.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.780
&gt;&gt; Okay, great, so
you mentioned Arbuckle Leadership Fellows.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:36.380
Maybe you can talk a little
bit about what that is, and

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:39.930
then both of you can also add on
other extracurriculars that you're

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:44.390
involved with, and other commitments that
you have outside of coursework and school.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:48.620
And also, how much time you actually
do spend on homework and classes,

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:49.300
relative to that.

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:51.088
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:10:51.088 --> 00:10:54.700
Yeah so, Arbuckle Fellows is
a coaching program that was started,

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:59.930
I want to say about ten years now, but I'm
not exactly sure if that's the right date.

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:04.250
And basically it allows the second
year class to coach and

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:09.020
lead the first years through
what we call Leadership Labs,

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:12.500
which is a first year required
class in your first quarter.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:16.530
That allows you to think about what
kind of leader you want to be if

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:18.890
you're the kind of leader you aren't,
how do you get there?

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:23.930
Learning from other leadership styles
that other students have that you

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:25.140
can sort of learn from.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:31.230
So you as an Arbuckle Fellow, lead six
students in that first quarter class and

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:36.840
then you switch into one on one
coaching for the winter term.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:41.460
And so you actually get assigned three
coachees that you work with each week for

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:44.570
an hour,
working through all sorts of topics.

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:49.350
So it ranges from leadership topics to
how do I balance my life at the GSB,

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:54.860
academics, and social, and career to
just personal stuff that's going on.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:55.720
So.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:58.210
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; That's been my most fulfilling

00:11:58.210 --> 00:11:59.380
activity at the GSB.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:01.000
A huge chunk of time.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:01.650
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.400
&gt;&gt; But a really great way to
practice those leadership and

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:07.040
coaching skills that I
came here to work on.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:08.120
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:10.330
Mike, how do you spend your
time outside of class?

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:13.170
Are there particular clubs that
you're really involved with?

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:14.780
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah I can talk to that.

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:20.535
So I spent a lot of my time
actually helping TA classes.

00:12:20.535 --> 00:12:21.380
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; At the GSB,

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:23.110
especially in the second year.

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:23.680
&gt;&gt; Interesting, yeah.

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:27.070
&gt;&gt; And, it's actually one of
the things that I've personally found,

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:31.980
kind of most rewarding about the Stanford
experience, which is that so

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:36.610
much of the learning that happens here,
happens because we have

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:41.380
just incredible classmates who come
from all types of different backgrounds.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:45.010
And so because I have a finance,
investing background,

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:49.560
I've spent a lot of my time,this
year helping two of the finance

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:54.840
professors here teach classes,
TA classes, develop case material.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:59.750
And that's sort of been my bug commitment
outside of regular coursework.

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:04.970
And it's something that I do because,
I think, one, it's good practice

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:09.890
in terms of learning how to be a leader,
how to communicate, how to teach people.

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:14.000
But at the same time it's allowed me
to give back to the community that

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:17.720
I think has already given me
a lot over the past two years.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:21.760
In terms of the split between time
spent on different activities,

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:26.190
I would say it varies pretty dramatically,
so

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:29.310
it's very hard to say what the average is,

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:35.020
because it's very unrepresentative
of the variants is very large.

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:38.460
And so I would say that
the great thing about the GSB,

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:44.320
is that If you come there is no
structure to which you have to adhere.

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:44.850
&gt;&gt; Mm hm.

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:48.240
&gt;&gt; If you want to spend a lot
of time on academics and

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:53.230
I feel like I've probably indexed on that
relatively more heavily than some of

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:55.370
are classmates, then you can do that.

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:59.770
If you want to spend more of
your time on extra curriculars,

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:04.080
more of your time on
even social activities,

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:09.305
that's completely accepted and
even encouraged at Stanford.

00:14:09.305 --> 00:14:13.570
I think it's really hard to say what
a typical GSB student looks like,

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:15.810
because there is no typical GSB student.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:18.010
Like everyone has different priorities and

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:22.020
all those priorities
are welcomed by the community.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:23.455
&gt;&gt; Yeah, choose your own adventure.

00:14:23.455 --> 00:14:24.740
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:27.210
&gt;&gt; So
we talked about learning in the classroom.

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:32.310
Could each of talk about one of the global
experiences or maybe multiple that you've

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:37.000
been involved with and
how that's shaped your GSB experience?

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:37.880
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:46.040
Yeah, so I participated in a GST, so a
Global Study Trip to Thailand last winter.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:49.320
And so the global study trips
are led by second years.

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:55.080
I was a first year at the time, so we were
taken to Thailand by five second years

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:59.860
who totally crafted the experience and
chose to look at the country through food.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:04.740
So we studied the food
supply chain in Thailand,

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:09.200
we visited a local rice patty, but we
also went and visited some of the largest

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:15.110
conglomerates that are actually packaging
and distributing all over the world.

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:20.430
And so, we just had a chance
to explore a new culture,

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:22.210
meet with all sorts of people.

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:25.670
Again, from someone running their own
small shop to someone running a huge

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:26.830
conglomerate.

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:29.050
And we're able to do it together.

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:30.510
So it was definitely for me,

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:35.540
I thought It allowed me to see
the country in a whole new way,

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:41.340
because we were very on the ground and
seeing it from all these different angles.

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:46.326
But also it really allowed me to forge
incredible friendships through just by

00:15:46.326 --> 00:15:49.701
being a country in a totally
new place with folks that

00:15:49.701 --> 00:15:52.478
I didn't know super well before the trip.

00:15:52.478 --> 00:15:57.675
And so I think I left with a whole new
appreciation for the country of Thailand,

00:15:57.675 --> 00:16:02.826
but also for a lot of my classmates who
I may not have otherwise gotten to know.

00:16:02.826 --> 00:16:03.569
Okay.

00:16:03.569 --> 00:16:05.612
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I had a similar experience.

00:16:05.612 --> 00:16:10.575
The trip that I remember most
vividly is actually one that was

00:16:10.575 --> 00:16:13.801
organized by our classmates to India.

00:16:13.801 --> 00:16:16.481
And we spent about ten days there,

00:16:16.481 --> 00:16:22.500
met with everyone ranging from political
leaders to small business owners.

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:27.960
To executives at larger corporations,
and learned a lot about India.

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:30.993
And developed a new sense of appreciation
for the amount of growth and

00:16:30.993 --> 00:16:32.630
potential in the country.

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:36.440
I think that stuff is,
you can get that stuff

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:39.340
just by going to different countries and
traveling on your own.

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:44.490
What's really unique about the trips that
you take at the GSB to these countries,

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:46.700
is that you're going with classmates.

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:50.230
I think something like 40% of our
class is international students.

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:54.318
And so, in all of these countries,
the people who are leading you,

00:16:54.318 --> 00:16:58.861
the people who are showing you
the country, are people from those places.

00:16:58.861 --> 00:17:01.895
Who either grew up there,
went to school there, or worked there.

00:17:01.895 --> 00:17:04.417
And have incredible networks, and

00:17:04.417 --> 00:17:08.710
just an incredible depth of
understanding of those places.

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:11.870
I think that's what's really
unique about these trips.

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:14.160
It's not the fact that you
can go all over the world,

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:19.000
it's the fact that you can go with someone
who knows those places intimately.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:24.380
That's been the most meaningful part
of the interactional experience for

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:25.630
me at the GSB.

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:28.977
&gt;&gt; Yeah, our trip leader to Thailand
actually was from Thailand.

00:17:28.977 --> 00:17:32.490
And so we had big family dinners with
her family who all live over there.

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:38.070
And got to meet leaders who we would
not otherwise have had access to.

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:41.713
So I think that's really a good point
about seeing the country through someone's

00:17:41.713 --> 00:17:43.893
eyes who's actually
experienced it day to day.

00:17:43.893 --> 00:17:50.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, great, so some of the exploration
happens through global experiences.

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:54.090
I think a lot of self exploration
also happens at the GSB.

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:58.921
And when you think about exploring your
career opportunities, have you used

00:17:58.921 --> 00:18:03.693
any specific resources at Stanford to
help you navigate that exploration?

00:18:03.693 --> 00:18:06.177
I know you mentioned possibly
doing a career change.

00:18:06.177 --> 00:18:13.700
So how has that been influenced by the GSB
and opportunities through the MBA program?

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:19.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say for me, it was mostly
those informational interviews that had

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:23.120
shaped that, and again,
access to the Alumni Network.

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:24.958
Also, we have a fantastic career center.

00:18:24.958 --> 00:18:30.031
So, I basically took those informational
interviews, had two buckets

00:18:30.031 --> 00:18:34.952
that I wanted to focus on for
my summer internship and for longer term.

00:18:34.952 --> 00:18:39.333
And the career center pulled up a whole
nother list of alums in those spaces,

00:18:39.333 --> 00:18:41.460
helped me think through a timeline.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:46.058
To actually think about trying
different things during the year.

00:18:46.058 --> 00:18:48.370
And I'm actually doing an internship, so

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:51.850
as I mentioned I'm a joint degree
student in ed and in business.

00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:57.053
And I'm actually doing an internship
with Stanford undergrads through

00:18:57.053 --> 00:19:02.000
the reslife department to fully
explore that higher ed opportunity.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:06.640
So that's possible,
you get course credits through the GSB.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:09.330
So I'm actually getting credit for
that internship.

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:13.682
And so you don't just have the summer
experience to explore this career change,

00:19:13.682 --> 00:19:16.058
but you also have time
throughout the year.

00:19:16.058 --> 00:19:18.334
I have classmates who
have started companies.

00:19:18.334 --> 00:19:21.029
I have classmates who have done 390s,

00:19:21.029 --> 00:19:24.964
what we call 390s similar to
this experience with res ed.

00:19:24.964 --> 00:19:28.357
And because we have
access to Silicon Valley,

00:19:28.357 --> 00:19:32.883
a lot of folks are pitching VC's and
working there day to day.

00:19:32.883 --> 00:19:36.692
So I think those both,
the Alumni Network, the Career Center and

00:19:36.692 --> 00:19:39.610
this internship day to day
have really helped me.

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:44.060
Solidify how to make that change, and
whether I want to make that change.

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:47.410
&gt;&gt; Okay, you touched on something
that we also have a question about,

00:19:47.410 --> 00:19:49.210
which is GSB location.

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:53.734
And Mike, I don't know if this has
come into your thought process for

00:19:53.734 --> 00:19:57.256
recruiting, or
the more formal channels of the GSB.

00:19:57.256 --> 00:20:01.504
Have you gotten a sense of how our
location in Silicon Valley has either

00:20:01.504 --> 00:20:03.168
expanded opportunities.

00:20:03.168 --> 00:20:09.020
Or the challenges that might come
with being on the west coast?

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:11.330
&gt;&gt; So I have a personal bias
towards the west coast,

00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:12.195
I've been here for-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:20:12.195 --> 00:20:13.742
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Almost ten years, and so

00:20:13.742 --> 00:20:16.600
that was actually a huge selling point for
me.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:22.320
For those of you who may not have been
here, the weather here is pretty awesome.

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:27.925
I would say that certainly
if you're in Palo Alto,

00:20:27.925 --> 00:20:32.775
but your desired industry and
company and firms are in New York City.

00:20:32.775 --> 00:20:36.015
There's no question that that
becomes logistically more difficult.

00:20:37.095 --> 00:20:42.795
Flying six hours out to New York,
flying back, is more challenging than

00:20:42.795 --> 00:20:46.069
for students at some of our
peer schools on the east coast.

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:52.790
That doesn't mean however, that those
firms aren't interested in GSB graduates.

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:57.380
In fact, a lot of the demand,
a lot of the job opportunities available

00:20:57.380 --> 00:20:59.960
through the CMC,
are from New York employers.

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:05.690
And so the down side to
the geography is not limited access,

00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:08.800
it's actually just logistical difficulty.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:11.280
And that's something I found when
I was interviewing in New York.

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.380
I was interviewing with
some investment firms.

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:16.811
And just flying out there was
the most painful part, but

00:21:16.811 --> 00:21:20.315
it wasn't hard to get them to
be interested in GSB grads.

00:21:20.315 --> 00:21:24.244
Obviously the benefit is
if you're interested in

00:21:24.244 --> 00:21:28.648
industries that are more
centered around the Bay Area.

00:21:28.648 --> 00:21:35.005
Biotech, information technology,
obviously, then there is, I think,

00:21:35.005 --> 00:21:41.030
the access and the logistical
ease is pretty unparalleled.

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:42.334
So, it really depends on what you want.

00:21:42.334 --> 00:21:48.927
But, I guess what I would emphasize,
is that access is incredibly broad.

00:21:48.927 --> 00:21:51.798
And that the one committment
you'd have to make

00:21:51.798 --> 00:21:56.560
is to undergo some more logistical
challenges in flying back and forth.

00:21:56.560 --> 00:22:03.310
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and building off that, I think,
I did recruit on the east coast full time.

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:08.860
And I think two things that actually
helped in being out here, surprisingly.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:10.670
One is that we have really long breaks.

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:15.330
So I would use those breaks to
network with folks in New York.

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:19.461
And really set up informational interviews
and interviews during those periods.

00:22:19.461 --> 00:22:23.073
And you do have, yes, logistical
nightmares during the school year.

00:22:23.073 --> 00:22:26.970
But if you're really strategic and
smart about how you use your time off.

00:22:26.970 --> 00:22:31.170
Stanford gives you a ton of time off
to be able to fly to other places.

00:22:31.170 --> 00:22:36.799
And then the second thing is,
recruiters are, I think, a little bit

00:22:36.799 --> 00:22:43.317
more surprised to get emails from folks
on the west coast about east coast jobs.

00:22:43.317 --> 00:22:46.870
And jobs in Chicago, and
jobs in Texas, and things like that.

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:51.059
So I think you actually have, or
I found that I actually had somewhat of

00:22:51.059 --> 00:22:55.831
an advantage, because they're like,
why would you want to leave California?

00:22:55.831 --> 00:23:01.450
[LAUGH] And so I found myself actually
recruiting with a smaller pool of people.

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:05.002
And so I think yes, you do have the access
if you want to stay in California.

00:23:05.002 --> 00:23:09.266
But if you don't, I think
the availability of breaks to network,

00:23:09.266 --> 00:23:13.853
and then also the fact that most people
do choose to stay in California.

00:23:13.853 --> 00:23:16.302
You have your bit of an anomaly,

00:23:16.302 --> 00:23:20.857
you actually have some increased
opportunity as a result.

00:23:20.857 --> 00:23:23.572
&gt;&gt; And I think also the size
of our program relatively,

00:23:23.572 --> 00:23:26.669
compared to the number of
opportunities on the east coast.

00:23:26.669 --> 00:23:29.997
It's just a supply and
demand perspective there's so

00:23:29.997 --> 00:23:33.120
few people I think relativly
in the larger scheme.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:37.188
That it creates a supply demand
advantage for our student body.

00:23:37.188 --> 00:23:40.448
And you brought up a really good example,
even with your significant other working

00:23:40.448 --> 00:23:42.560
in real estate in New York
having access to our alums.

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:46.260
So I also think about networks
more broadly than geography.

00:23:46.260 --> 00:23:50.225
I think a lot about networks in
2016 being informational networks,

00:23:50.225 --> 00:23:53.150
and access to people who
really have deep expertise.

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:57.380
And I think so much of that,
of course you want contextual knowledge,

00:23:57.380 --> 00:24:01.683
but also learning about The access
to information that you have through

00:24:01.683 --> 00:24:07.270
our alums can really cross borders and
time zones pretty easily these days.

00:24:07.270 --> 00:24:08.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for sure.

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:10.860
&gt;&gt; So, while logistics can be a challenge,

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:14.720
we also have a question about
challenges in general at Stanford.

00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:16.400
And someone's asking us,

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:20.059
what has been your most difficult
experience at Stanford?

00:24:23.461 --> 00:24:24.750
It's a head scratcher.

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:25.560
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:27.500
&gt;&gt; I can tell you about mine.

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:34.670
So, I'm actually at Stanford with my
partner and we applied at the same time.

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:38.660
We were both really
fortunate to be admitted.

00:24:38.660 --> 00:24:42.210
And she's also an MBA too.

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:48.730
And the biggest challenge for us just on a
personal level was just in the beginning,

00:24:48.730 --> 00:24:53.422
the GSB is so all consuming in terms of

00:24:53.422 --> 00:24:58.365
its demands on your time from
the academics to the social scene.

00:24:58.365 --> 00:25:00.700
To, you're thinking about jobs and

00:25:00.700 --> 00:25:04.380
internships and
should I start a business etc., etc.

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:09.353
And I think the biggest challenge for
us and a source of friction in our

00:25:09.353 --> 00:25:14.670
relationship in those early days coming
to GSB was how much time do we spend

00:25:14.670 --> 00:25:20.448
on spending time with each other versus
on these other priorities that we have?

00:25:20.448 --> 00:25:24.545
Because we do want to go through the
transformative experience that the GSB has

00:25:24.545 --> 00:25:25.800
to offer.

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:28.450
And I think it generalizes
to a broader point, for

00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:33.650
me, it manifested itself in my
relationship with my significant other.

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:38.730
I think for people who don't have
partners here, the generalization is that

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:43.370
you have your personal life, you have your
life that you led before the GSB and now

00:25:43.370 --> 00:25:47.560
you have these massive time commitments
that are put on you by the GSB.

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:53.080
And the question of how you balance those
two can be fraught with uncertainty,

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:57.860
and can cause frictions with
your relationships with people,

00:25:57.860 --> 00:26:00.260
before you came.

00:26:00.260 --> 00:26:04.150
I think everyone I know has successfully
worked through those, my partner and

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:07.980
I, we're able to find the right balance,
but

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:10.230
in the beginning that can
be a really big challenge.

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:13.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
just building off of that I think,

00:26:13.580 --> 00:26:17.810
I think my biggest challenge was
the transition fall quarter as well.

00:26:17.810 --> 00:26:21.350
For slightly different reasons, I think I
came in knowing what I wanted to focus on.

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:25.970
But the second you get here,
you're exposed to so much and

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:27.820
you're exposed to so
many different classmates.

00:26:27.820 --> 00:26:30.560
And you hear about a company that
someone's been at and you're like, wow,

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:34.020
that sounds interesting, maybe I'll
add a third industry to look at.

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:35.550
And so I think, for me,

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:39.750
it was really figuring out,
how do I learn to say no to things, and

00:26:39.750 --> 00:26:43.680
be comfortable getting out of the
experience what I came here to get out?

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:48.499
And so I would say the more clear you
can be about what you want from business

00:26:48.499 --> 00:26:52.697
school, and the closer you can
stick to that when you're here,

00:26:52.697 --> 00:26:54.830
the easier that adjustment is.

00:26:54.830 --> 00:26:58.196
But I would say, yeah,
that the first quarter at the GSB,

00:26:58.196 --> 00:27:02.869
there's a lot going on and there's a lot
of opportunity, it's really critical

00:27:02.869 --> 00:27:07.305
to focus on what you decide that you
want to get out of the experience.

00:27:07.305 --> 00:27:10.652
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's perfect, I really
appreciate you adding some advice in

00:27:10.652 --> 00:27:14.442
there, because I was going to ask if you
had any final tips, so that's really great

00:27:14.442 --> 00:27:17.763
words to live by, not just for
the application, but I think for life.

00:27:17.763 --> 00:27:23.419
Mike, any final thoughts on tips
coming into either applying to or

00:27:23.419 --> 00:27:25.145
attending the GSB?

00:27:25.145 --> 00:27:27.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely.

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:34.140
So my final word of advice for the guests
who are watching over the internet,

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:39.270
is that a lot of the admissions process,

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:43.450
and even the GSB process itself,
if you haven't been a part of it,

00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:46.780
if you haven't experienced it yet,
it can seem contrived.

00:27:46.780 --> 00:27:51.100
It can seem like, what do they actually
mean by being introspective and

00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:51.850
being reflective?

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:55.100
Aren't those just buzz-words, right?

00:27:55.100 --> 00:27:56.400
&gt;&gt; Right, yeah.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:28:00.870
&gt;&gt; And what I would say is,
they can be, and they can be,

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:04.780
that only happens if you
don't truly kind of buy in.

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:07.990
If you don't truly commit yourself
to being reflective, right?

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:10.670
To thinking about why have you
made the decisions you did?

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:11.870
Being honest about that.

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:17.050
Being honest about where you're from,
who you are, what your goals are,

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:22.210
instead of what you think that society or
admissions officers want to see from you.

00:28:22.210 --> 00:28:24.990
Or, if you're admitted,
what you think your peers or

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:27.100
your professors want to see from you.

00:28:27.100 --> 00:28:31.830
The only way you really get
to experience the GSB's

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:37.200
benefits at a true level of depth is
when you buy in, is when you say, I

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:41.710
am going to do these things even though it
feels kind of awkward and cheesy at first.

00:28:41.710 --> 00:28:46.360
I am going to reflect, I am going to
think about my life as it's been so

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:49.640
far, and what I hope for
it to be like in the future.

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:52.530
So that would be my final word of advice,

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:56.320
and the buzzword I would use to
capture that is be authentic.

00:28:56.320 --> 00:28:57.490
Be authentic to yourself.

00:28:58.870 --> 00:29:02.860
&gt;&gt; Those are great tips, both for
the application and for life.

00:29:02.860 --> 00:29:05.480
I wanted to thank you both so
much for taking the time.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:09.390
I know you always have 50 things you
could be doing any half hour of the day.

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:13.440
So I really appreciate it and
you really brought the GSB to life.

00:29:13.440 --> 00:29:14.850
So, thank you so much.

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:18.370
All of you that are joining us,
I want to thank you for your time as well.

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:22.440
Really appreciate you taking the time
to learn more about the MBA program.

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:27.270
As a reminder you can always learn
more about the GSB on our website,

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:30.160
and we'll also be on the road this summer.

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:33.030
So if you want to learn more
about Stanford in person,

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:35.330
you can take a look at our
event calendar online.

